bisection returns the same minimum accepted grid budget as enumeration.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
\Cref{lem:5-buffer,prop:5-local-coverage,prop:5-shared-bound} give exact
primary top-$c$ under the common key $(\primarydist,\mathrm{position})$.
Its prefixes nest as $c$ grows. Fixed refinement/final order supplies \cref{prop:6-monotonicity}'s remaining premises.
\end{proof}
\end{minipage}\par\medskip

The corollary covers 2,176 draws; 128 OpenAI-3072/$S=128$ draws use distance-only selection. All 2,304 recorded curves are monotone and match enumeration (\cref{sec:7-budget-trace}); this is finite evidence.

\paragraph{Full-pool coverage.}
Two FULL cells group Vogue; ten use $\gscan=0$. All twelve match enumeration. Ten distance-only cells remain outside \cref{cor:6-grouped-calibration}: nesting needs a common cutoff order or no crossing ties. A matching held-out path does not ensure this.

\paragraph{A rank-based extension.}
Under the same nesting/fixed-score premises, one-based primary ranks give
\begin{equation}
 D_S(\alpha)=\sum_{q\in\mathcal Q_S}\sum_{x\in\mathcal R_{\rm ref}(q)}
 \mathbf1\{\operatorname{rank}_{\rm P}(x)>c(\alpha)\}.
 \label{eq:6-rank-curve}
\end{equation}
Exporting reference ranks could replace repeated budget searches. This is unimplemented.

